<div style="background:#fff;"><div class="cap-section"><div style="margin-bottom:8px;"><div style="margin-bottom:26px;"><h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:28px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink);line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:-0.02em;margin:0;">Frequently asked questions</h2></div>
    <div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--radius-card);overflow:hidden;background:#fff;">
      <details class="sc-faq" open>
      <summary>What is the Joint Development Model?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">JDM is a collaborative development model in which customer and Hana engineering teams define requirements and develop the product and manufacturing process together, with a common roadmap from prototype to volume.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>How is JDM different from a normal build-to-print program?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">A build-to-print program begins with a substantially completed customer design. In JDM, manufacturing engineering is involved earlier, allowing product design and production process decisions to develop in parallel.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>Who owns the IP in a joint program?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">IP ownership is defined in the program agreement. Customer information and IP are protected under NDA and secured through Hana’s ISO 27001 framework.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>Is JDM only for large programs?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">No fixed program size is specified. JDM is most relevant where product and manufacturing decisions are closely connected and both parties expect an ongoing engineering relationship.</div>
    </details>
    </div>
